const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'What is the financial analyst career path in India, step by step?',
    a: 'Most people start as a Junior or Trainee Financial Analyst (0-2 years), move to Financial Analyst (2-4 years) once they can own a report or model independently, then to Senior Financial Analyst (4-7 years) once they lead complex analysis and mentor juniors. From there the path splits toward Finance Manager or FP&A Manager (7-10 years) managing a small team, and eventually Associate Director or Director of Finance (10-15+ years) shaping strategy for a business unit or region. The exact titles vary by company, but this analyst-to-senior-analyst-to-manager-to-director shape holds across FP&A, corporate finance, equity research, and credit analysis.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do you need an MBA or CFA to become a financial analyst in India?',
    a: 'No. A BCom, BBA, or B.Sc in Finance is enough to start as a junior financial analyst at most Indian companies, provided you can demonstrate real Excel and financial-statement-reading skill. An MBA accelerates the move to senior analyst or manager-level roles, especially at larger companies and GCCs. A CFA charter is most valuable for equity research, asset management, and investment-decision-heavy roles, and is not required for FP&A or corporate finance analyst positions, though it strengthens any finance resume.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the real salary progression for a financial analyst in India?',
    a: 'Entry-level financial analysts (under 1 year) typically earn Rs 3.5-6 LPA. Early career analysts (1-4 years) move to roughly Rs 5.5-9 LPA. Mid-career Senior Financial Analysts (4-8 years) commonly reach Rs 9-18 LPA, rising toward Rs 18-35 LPA for Finance Manager to Director-level roles at 8-15 years. Pay varies significantly by sector — GCCs and equity research/asset management roles tend to pay more than standard IT-services or domestic-company FP&A roles at the same experience level.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the difference between FP&A, equity research, corporate finance, and credit analyst roles?',
    a: 'FP&A analysts work inside a company building budgets, forecasts, and variance reports for internal decision-making. Equity research analysts study public companies or sectors and publish buy/sell/hold recommendations for investors, which legally requires SEBI\'s NISM-Series-XV certification in India. Corporate finance analysts evaluate capital projects, M&A targets, and capital structure decisions inside a company. Credit analysts assess whether a borrower, company, or bond issuer can repay its debt, working at rating agencies, banks, or NBFCs. All four use overlapping core skills (financial modelling, Excel, valuation) but differ meaningfully in daily rhythm, regulatory requirements, and who you report to.',
  },
