    q: 'Is a financial analyst career better than investment banking in India?',
    a: 'It depends on what you are optimising for. A financial analyst career in most sectors offers meaningfully calmer and more predictable hours — typically 45-55 hours a week with occasional busier close or forecast weeks — compared to investment banking\'s 70-85 hour norm with occasional 90-100+ hour crunches. Investment banking analyst pay at bulge-bracket firms is generally higher at the entry level, but financial analyst roles, especially inside GCCs, offer strong pay growth, faster promotion cycles in some cases, and a substantially more sustainable long-term lifestyle. If deal intensity and prestige matter more to you than hours, see our full guide on how to start a career in investment banking in India.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is AI going to replace financial analyst jobs in India?',
    a: 'AI is automating the repetitive layer of the job — data reconciliation, first-pass variance flagging, basic report generation — faster than replacing the analyst role outright. The financial analysts who stay competitive are the ones who treat directing and auditing AI output as a core skill, then spend the time saved on judgment calls, business partnering, and communicating financial insight to non-finance stakeholders, which remain much harder for AI to replace convincingly.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What certifications actually help a financial analyst career in India?',
    a: 'CFA is the strongest global credential for equity research, asset management, and investment-decision roles, though it takes 3-4 years including work-experience requirements. NISM-Series-XV Research Analyst certification is a legal SEBI requirement if your work involves publishing equity research. FMVA or similar financial-modelling certifications (roughly Rs 20,000-80,000 in India) teach practical modelling mechanics faster than self-study and can strengthen a resume, but none of these credentials alone gets you hired — they need to be paired with a real, defensible financial model you can walk an interviewer through.',
